ncz-screensavers (0.8.15) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ncz-screensaver-idled honours the Singularity "Lock on Suspend" setting
    (dev.sinty.lockscreen lock-on-suspend) as well as its own key: with it
    off, the daemon no longer locks the session when the system sleeps.
  * When the Singularity shell locks before sleep itself (it then holds a
    logind "sleep" delay inhibitor as "Singularity"), the daemon leaves the
    sleep lock to the shell, so resume shows a single lock screen. Without
    that inhibitor the daemon still locks before sleep.
  * Regression tests in launcher/tests/test-idled-dryrun.sh.

 -- Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:30:00 -0400

ncz-screensavers (0.8.14) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The lock-hosted screensaver keeps animating until someone uses the
    machine. On NCZ-OS the lock hosts the idle screensaver, so the old
    default (freeze 15 seconds after the saver started) stopped every idle
    screensaver on a frame with nobody there. The default freeze mode is now
    "prompt": no timer; the lock freezes the saver only when input raises the
    unlock prompt. NCZ_SAVER_FREEZE=always keeps the intro-then-freeze
    behaviour as an opt-in, "auto" applies it to Apple T2 Macs only, and an
    unknown value means "prompt". --print-policy now prints freeze=prompt.
  * Savers that draw their final pass to the window (the classics with
    post-processing such as Cubestorm and Noof, and Magmasimplex) showed a
    black screen behind the lock. Off-screen, framebuffer 0 is now the lock's
    frame buffer, so they render there.
  * Magmasimplex renders on Arm Mali (Sky1): its shader now starts at the
    #version line, which the Mali compiler requires.
  * Regression tests: launcher/tests/test-feed-engines.sh (visible, changing
    frames off-screen for a set of savers), a no-timer case in
    test-feed-freeze.sh and the new defaults in test-feed-policy.py.

 -- Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:00:00 -0400

ncz-screensavers (0.8.13) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ncz-screensaver-idled (delegated mode) no longer asks the shell to lock at
    hack-idle-delay. The SHELL is now the single idle-lock owner (the image's
    post-install hook already sets dev.sinty.lockscreen:idle-delay to match
    dev.ncz.screensaver:hack-idle-delay), so two lock clients are no longer
    started at the same instant and the lock's frame source is no longer
    clobbered. Suspend locking and DPMS stay with the daemon. As a result, the
    screensaver actually renders behind the lock at default timings instead
    of failing closed with no frames.
  * ncz-screensavers-plugin's desktop file (screensaver.plugin) is now
    declared app-shipped (X-Singularity-App=dev.sinty.desktop). The
    Settings > Plugins > "Screensaver and Lockscreen" page is populated as
    soon as the plugin is discovered, independent of when the postinst
    gschema override (or ncz-saver ensure-plugin at login) adds the
    "screensaver" entry to dev.sinty.desktop enabled-plugins. Settings pane
    is no longer "No settings available" after a clean first login.
  * Regression tests:
    - launcher/tests/test-idled-dryrun.sh asserts the new delegated plan
      shape (no idle lock timer in delegated mode; suspend locking stays).
    - tools/test-plugin-settings-pane.py reproduces headlessly what the
      shell's PluginManager does and asserts the settings pane is populated.
  * Package version bumped to 0.8.13; the installed-target verifier's
    compiled-in policy defaults are unchanged (lock_fps=8, render_div=2,
    freeze=always, freeze_after_s=15, battery_lock_fps=6).

 -- Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>  Mon, 13 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400

ncz-screensavers (0.8.12) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Lock-hosted saver (ncz-screensaver-feed): cool-by-default operation. While
    the screen is locked the saver now runs a short intro at a low, capped
    frame rate (8 fps, half resolution) and then freezes on every system,
    handing the lock its last frame to keep. A thermal governor pauses
    rendering if a sensor approaches ~85 C and resumes when it cools. The
    effect is a lock screen that sits near the idle power floor instead of
    driving the GPU and CPU hard for as long as the screen is locked.
  * The freeze is fail-closed and portable: the feed freezes only with a lock
    that understands the HOLD message. Against an older lock (built before
    HOLD existed) the feed instead keeps the low frame rate and the thermal
    governor and never sends HOLD, so it degrades quietly rather than
    faulting.
  * Off-screen renderer: on the CIX Mali blob (arm64) the frame source adds a
    GBM EGL platform, so the lock-hosted saver animates on Mali GPUs instead
    of showing a static frame. Mesa/AMD/Intel still use the surfaceless path
    unchanged.
  * `ncz-screensaver-feed --print-policy` prints the compiled-in policy
    defaults (lock frame rate, render divisor, freeze mode and timer, battery
    frame rate) for the image's installed-target verifier.

 -- Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:35:00 -0400

ncz-screensavers (0.8.11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ncz-screensaver-feed: the producer half of the NCZ lock-hosted
    screensaver. When the NCZ lockscreen is built with its out-of-process
    frame-source hook and SINGULARITY_LOCK_RENDERER names this command, the
    session lock presents screensaver frames instead of a static prompt. The
    feed reuses the launcher catalog/plan logic and runs each hack off-screen
    (every hack binary gains an NCZ_FEED_SOCKET mode that renders into an FBO
    through EGL surfaceless or a device platform, with no compositor).
  * ncz-screensaver-idled: delegated mode. When /etc/ncz/lock-renderer.env
    names a renderer, the daemon no longer starts its own wlr-layer-shell
    saver; at hack-idle-delay it asks the shell to lock (ScreenSaver.Lock)
    and the lock hosts the saver.

